      "text": "To evaluate \\(\\log_3 (27\\sqrt{3})\\), we start by expressing the argument of the logarithm in terms of powers of 3. First, we recognize that \\(27\\) can be written as \\(3^3\\). Therefore, we can rewrite the expression as follows:\n\n\\[\n27\\sqrt{3} = 3^3 \\cdot \\sqrt{3}\n\\]\n\nNext, we use the property of logarithms that \\(\\log_b (xy) = \\log_b x + \\log_b y\\). Applying this property, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\log_3 (27\\sqrt{3}) = \\log_3 (3^3 \\cdot \\sqrt{3}) = \\log_3 (3^3 \\cdot 3^{1/2})\n\\]\n\nWe can combine the exponents of 3 using the property \\(\\log_b (a^m \\cdot b^n) = \\log_b a^m + \\log_b b^n = m \\log_b a + n\\):\n\n\\[\n\\log_3 (3^3 \\cdot 3^{1/2}) = \\log_3 (3^3) + \\log_3 (3^{1/2}) = 3 \\log_3 3 + \\frac{1}{2} \\log_3 3\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\log_3 3 = 1\\), we substitute this value in:\n\n\\[\n3 \\log_3 3 + \\frac{1}{2} \\log_3 3 = 3 \\cdot 1 + \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 1 = 3 + \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{6}{2} + \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{7}{2}\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(\\log_3 (27\\sqrt{3})\\) is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{7}{2}}\\).",
